{"metadata": {"team_id": "uva", "run_id": "disco-qrecc", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "1-1_7", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Acid reflux, often experienced as heartburn, occurs when stomach acid flows back into the esophagus. This backflow is primarily due to a relaxed lower esophageal sphincter (LES), the valve between the esophagus and the stomach. Normally, the LES stays closed, preventing stomach contents from traveling upwards. However, several factors can cause it to relax, leading to acid reflux.  One reason is the composition of stomach acid itself. Insufficient stomach acid can lead to a poorly functioning LES, as it's the acidity that signals the valve to remain tight. When there's not enough acid, the LES may loosen, allowing acid to escape into the esophagus.  Additionally, lifestyle factors contribute to acid reflux. Eating late at night, lying down soon after a meal, being overweight, or consuming trigger foods like spicy or fatty foods can increase the likelihood of experiencing reflux. Pressure on the LES, such as from lying down, also encourages acid to move upward.  Understanding these factors can help in managing and preventing acid reflux symptoms. Adjusting meal times, maintaining a healthy weight, and avoiding known trigger foods are practical steps to consider.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0022-52-08268:12": 16.14864730834961, "clueweb22-en0002-87-15707:6": 15.656782150268555, "clueweb22-en0004-92-06401:8": 15.295058250427246, "clueweb22-en0006-71-01643:18": 15.281026840209961, "clueweb22-en0002-03-19296:6": 14.916516304016113, "clueweb22-en0030-64-11346:13": 14.819597244262695, "clueweb22-en0003-49-04824:18": 14.714767456054688, "clueweb22-en0007-10-18426:18": 14.554615020751953, "clueweb22-en0023-27-00769:8": 14.55236530303955, "clueweb22-en0032-97-12085:4": 14.547182083129883}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I eat dinner late at night.", "I have gained weight recently because I stopped exercising for the last 6 months.", "I want to chang