{"metadata": {"team_id": "uva", "run_id": "disco-qrecc", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_3", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Understanding finger anatomy might help you identify where the pain originates. Your fingers consist of three bones connected by joints, which are supported and stabilized by ligaments. The flexor tendons run along the palm side of the fingers, attached to muscles in your forearms. These tendons help bend your fingers and are encased in connective tissue sheaths, forming pulleys labeled A1 to A5. For climbers, pain commonly arises from the A2 or A4 pulley due to the strain from crimps and gripping holds.  Signs of a potential tendon or pulley injury include swelling, pain when bending your finger, a limited range of motion, tenderness along the palm side, and possibly numbness. Since you're climbing regularly and have a history of bouldering, it's important to monitor these symptoms. Climbing can put significant stress on these structures, potentially causing overuse injuries.   Consider reducing your climbing intensity to allow healing, and consult a healthcare professional if the pain persists or worsens. They can provide a precise diagnosis and recommend a treatment plan to prevent further injury. Additionally, you might explore strengthening exercises to support your fingers and reduce stress during climbing.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:0": 12.461442947387695, "clueweb22-en0021-66-06454:13": 12.336593627929688, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 12.239924430847168, "clueweb22-en0013-93-15759:18": 12.180508613586426, "clueweb22-en0016-25-19413:13": 12.180508613586426, "clueweb22-en0038-00-07527:13": 12.16703987121582, "clueweb22-en0040-90-14264:16": 12.033400535583496, "clueweb22-en0008-84-09982:22": 12.0237455368042, "clueweb22-en0014-41-15217:6": 12.012500762939453, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 11.925185203552246}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.", "I climb outdoors.", "Many yea