{"metadata": {"team_id": "uva", "run_id": "disco-qrecc", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_4", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Based on your symptoms and climbing background, it sounds like you could potentially be dealing with a flexor tendon or pulley injury, commonly referred to as \"climber's finger.\" Pain in the tendon area can result from overuse or strain, especially if you're frequently using small holds or engaging in crimping grips while climbing. If the pain is located around the finger's palm side, specifically near the A2 or A4 pulley, it might be due to inflammation or damage to the tendon sheath or pulleys.  If it's a flexor tendon injury, you might experience an inability to bend one or more joints of your finger, tenderness, and possibly swelling in the affected area. Numbness can occur if there's nerve involvement. Pain when bending your finger suggests that the tendon or its sheath could be irritated or damaged.  It's crucial to rest the finger and avoid climbing until the pain subsides to prevent worsening the condition. Consulting a healthcare professional for an accurate diagnosis and tailored treatment plan, including potential therapeutic exercises or splinting, is recommended.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 10.606542587280273, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2": 10.102832794189453, "clueweb22-en0014-41-15217:6": 10.050553321838379, "clueweb22-en0008-84-09982:22": 10.021124839782715, "clueweb22-en0013-93-15759:18": 9.920543670654297, "clueweb22-en0016-25-19413:13": 9.920543670654297, "clueweb22-en0038-00-07527:13": 9.915127754211426, "clueweb22-en0017-96-08323:0": 9.6365327835083, "clueweb22-en0015-71-10781:17": 9.548286437988281, "clueweb22-en0001-34-18428:18": 9.498675346374512}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.", "I climb outdoors.", "Many years ago I had a bad fall while bouldering."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:1": 10.606542587280273, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:2":