{"metadata": {"team_id": "uva", "run_id": "disco-qrecc", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_8", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "When you're ready to start climbing again after taking time off for your finger injury, it's essential to ease back into it gradually to prevent re-injury. Begin by focusing on finger strengthening and mobility exercises to rebuild strength and flexibility. Implement warm-up routines that include dynamic stretches and finger-specific exercises before each climbing session. Consider using tape to provide additional support to the affected finger, especially around the joints and pulleys. Start with climbs that are well below your previous limit and emphasize technique over strength to avoid putting extra strain on your fingers.  Incorporate rest days into your schedule to allow your fingers to recover, and listen to your body\u2014if you feel any significant pain, stop and rest. Maintain open communication with your climbing partners about your recovery status, so they can support you in making smart climbing choices. Lastly, consider discussing your progress with a physiotherapist who specializes in hand or sports injuries, to ensure you\u2019re on the right track for a healthy return to climbing. Remember, gradual progression and patience are key in avoiding setbacks.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:7": 8.3729829788208, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:7": 8.244682312011719, "clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:6": 8.073542594909668, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:8": 8.029108047485352, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:9": 7.952752113342285, "clueweb22-en0028-52-13658:3": 7.241605281829834, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:5": 7.202380657196045, "clueweb22-en0014-37-10189:19": 7.125335216522217, "clueweb22-en0014-37-10189:18": 7.082612991333008, "clueweb22-en0016-21-05173:13": 7.0652384757995605}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.", "I climb outdoors."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0012-60-11476:7": 8.3729829788208,