{"metadata": {"team_id": "uva", "run_id": "disco-qrecc", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "3-1_11", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "Preventative taping of healthy fingers is generally not recommended. Research indicates that taping fingers that are not injured may not provide meaningful support and can even lead to injury by preventing the natural stress and strengthening needed for the pulleys. It's important to allow your healthy fingers to function naturally so they can keep pace with muscle growth and adapt to climbing stress.   However, if you're recovering from an injury and feel that specific movements might strain other fingers, you could consider taping them temporarily, but primarily focus on using correct climbing techniques. If you're concerned about your other fingers during recovery, gentle stretching and strengthening exercises might be more beneficial for overall hand health rather than taping them unnecessarily.  When you do tape an injured finger, ensure you use a proper technique like the H-taping method to provide the best support to the injured pulley while still allowing some controlled movement. This approach, combined with gradually increasing your climbing intensity as you heal, will help in a safer return to your regular climbing activities.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:10": 15.673283576965332, "clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:9": 15.476041793823242, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:9": 15.252213478088379, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:13": 15.007638931274414, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:6": 14.604145050048828, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:14": 14.602338790893555, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:8": 14.170758247375488, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:2": 13.69113540649414, "clueweb22-en0031-25-08597:3": 13.378851890563965, "clueweb22-en0033-87-09727:12": 13.104934692382812}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I am climbing regularly for the past 3 years.", "I climb outdoors."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0007-15-09796:10": 15.673283576965332, "clueweb22-en00