{"metadata": {"team_id": "uva", "run_id": "disco-qrecc", "run_type": "automatic"}, "turn_id": "9-2_7", "responses": [{"rank": 1, "text": "No worries! Let's use a simpler method to find the right chain length. Here's a straightforward approach:  1. Remove the chain from the derailleur and wrap it around the largest chainring in the front and the largest cog in the back without threading it through the derailleur.  2. Pull the chain tight and identify the point where the two ends overlap.   3. Add two extra links to this point. This allows for proper tension when the chain is routed through the derailleur.  4. Cut the chain at this point and reconnect it.   This method ensures that your chain is neither too tight nor too loose. If you have your old chain and it's the correct length, you could also use it as a reference to measure the new chain. This should help you get your bike running smoothly again! If you need more guidance, feel free to ask.", "citations": {"clueweb22-en0007-14-08949:6": 11.269262313842773, "clueweb22-en0014-52-02554:9": 11.218009948730469, "clueweb22-en0007-14-08949:3": 11.02152156829834, "clueweb22-en0014-52-02554:8": 10.888015747070312, "clueweb22-en0009-91-00148:5": 10.312736511230469, "clueweb22-en0012-54-17197:3": 10.101668357849121, "clueweb22-en0020-85-09566:9": 10.006298065185547, "clueweb22-en0008-26-01793:7": 9.909936904907227, "clueweb22-en0009-91-00148:2": 9.843679428100586, "clueweb22-en0007-14-08949:5": 9.756433486938477}, "ptkb_provenance": ["I sometimes get overwhelmed by too many technical choices.", "I am not really handy with DIY."]}], "references": {"clueweb22-en0007-14-08949:6": 11.269262313842773, "clueweb22-en0014-52-02554:9": 11.218009948730469, "clueweb22-en0007-14-08949:3": 11.02152156829834, "clueweb22-en0014-52-02554:8": 10.888015747070312, "clueweb22-en0009-91-00148:5": 10.312736511230469, "clueweb22-en0012-54-17197:3": 10.101668357849121, "clueweb22-en0020-85-09566:9": 10.006298065185547, "clueweb22-en0008-26-01793: